            <ArticleTitle>The effects of hydroxy-methyl-glutaryl co-enzyme A reductase inhibitors on platelet thrombus formation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>301-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Hydroxy-methyl-glutaryl co-enzyme A reductase inhibitors (HMG CoA RIs) markedly improve the lipid profile of patients with hypercholesterolemia, but the magnitude and time course of the effect of these drugs on other risk factors for atherosclerosis are not well defined.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We employed a random assignment, double-blind design to compare the effect of 8 weeks of HMG CoA RI therapy with either pravastatin (40 mg QD; n=12) or simvastatin (20 mg QD; n=12) with placebo (n=13) on serum lipids, platelet thrombus formation (PTF), and markers of inflammation and thrombosis in patients with coronary artery disease. PTF was measured using a validated ex vivo perfusion chamber system.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Total and LDL cholesterol decreased 20.3 +/- 12.7% and 31.4 +/- 16.5% in the HMG CoA RI group and were unchanged with placebo (P&lt;0.01). Triglycerides also decreased 15.3 +/- 22.5% with HMG CoA RI therapy, but increased 8.4 +/- 30.0% with placebo (P=0.01). PTF increased 54.1 +/- 89.0% with placebo and decreased 8.0 +/- 46.82% with HMG CoA RI treatment (P&lt;0.01).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">HMG CoA RI therapy with pravastatin or simvastatin reduces PTF after only 8 weeks of therapy. Such lipid effects may contribute to the prompt reduction in cardiovascular events noted in some clinical trials.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
